    "message": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "content": "I shipped a feature nobody used. It still stings.\n\nTwo years ago, my team kept hearing the same request: \"We need better reporting.\"\n\nSo we built it. A beautiful dashboard. Filters, exports, scheduled emails, the works. We polished it for 6 weeks and launched it with a big announcement.\n\nCrickets.\n\nAfter a month, 3% had tried it once. 0% stuck around. When we finally sat down with users, we heard the painful truth:\n\nWhen they said \"more reporting,\" they meant \"just give me the answer.\" Not another chart to interpret.\n\nWe had solved our interpretation of the request, not their actual job-to-be-done.\n\nThat flop changed how I build forever:\n\n**1. Fall in love with the problem, not the solution.**\nWe spent 6 weeks building and 0 days shadowing users. Now I won't let my team design anything until we've watched 5 users struggle with the problem live.\n\n**2. Ship the embarrassing version first.**\nWe could have validated value in 2 days with a manual weekly email with insights. Instead we built automation for a workflow nobody wanted.\n\n**3. Define failure upfront.**\nWe never agreed on: \"If X% don't retain in Y weeks, we kill it.\" So the zombie feature lived in our codebase for a year, adding maintenance cost and guilt.\n\nThe most expensive code is the code nobody uses. The best PMs I know aren't the best builders — they're the fastest learners.\n\nHave you ever shipped something nobody used? What did it teach you?\n\n👇 I’d love to hear your story.\n\n#ProductManagement #Startups #Leadership #BuildInPublic",
     "refusal": null,
